Date: Wed, 19 Aug 2026 00:45:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 6/6] extractor: exclude point-in-time measurements, keep the
 mechanism

0005 stopped the extractor storing hypotheses, live state and discussion
mechanics. Two independent runs afterwards still leaked the remaining family --
measurements. "There are currently 86 stale copies" (importance 8) and "The fleet
holds 571 rows" (importance 5). The first was already false by the time it was
reported: a sweep and a prune had both moved the count.

A measurement slips past the earlier rules because it is neither a guess nor
obviously in-flight; it is simply true, briefly. The durable thing is always the
mechanism that produced the number.

Deliberately paired with an explicit KEEP rule for structural numbers -- hardware
counts, ports, caps, thresholds, versions -- so the exclusion cannot swallow the
facts that describe how the system is built.
---
--- a/src/hippocampai/pipeline/extractor.py
+++ b/src/hippocampai/pipeline/extractor.py
@@ -61,9 +61,31 @@
    what someone is about to do next.
 10. PRESERVE NEGATION EXACTLY. "X does not run on Y" must never become "X runs on Y".
     If a statement is negated, conditional, or hedged, keep that or drop it entirely.
-11. If nothing in the conversation is durable, return an empty array. An empty result
+11. Measurements and verification results taken AT A MOMENT: counts, tallies, sweep
+    findings, test outcomes, "there are currently N", "X now holds N rows", "zero Y
+    remain", "all checks passed". These are true when observed and stale the next
+    time anyone looks. Store the MECHANISM that produced the number, never the
+    number. This applies even when a trusted colleague states the figure plainly —
+    a confident report of a count is still a count.
+12. DO keep numbers that are STRUCTURAL rather than momentary: hardware counts, port
+    numbers, configured limits, caps, thresholds, versions. Those describe how the
+    system is built, not what it happened to contain at one instant. "The listing
+    endpoint caps at 5000" is durable; "the listing returned 571 rows" is not.
+13. If nothing in the conversation is durable, return an empty array. An empty result
     is correct and expected — a wrong memory is far worse than a missing one, because
     it gets injected into every future conversation as if it were true.
+
+MORE EXAMPLES OF WHAT NOT TO EXTRACT:
+User: "there are currently 86 stale copies in the system"
+  -> nothing (a count; it moved before the day was out)
+User: "the fleet namespace holds 571 rows"
+  -> nothing as stated. If the point being made was that the listing endpoint
+     silently caps, store THAT: {"text": "The memory listing endpoint caps its
+     result set, so a caller must request more rows than it expects", ...}
+User: "zero contaminated tags remain in any namespace"
+  -> nothing (a verification result, true only at that moment)
+User: "the epyc box has 4 GPUs and the embedder listens on port 5010"
+  -> KEEP BOTH (structural, not momentary)
 
 EXAMPLES OF WHAT NOT TO EXTRACT:
 User: "maybe the vectors were never re-embedded, that could explain it"
